### pubmed_34942852

Research on population-based studies around the world has reported that the prevalence estimates of individuals with [cerebral palsy](DISORDER:DIS008) ([CP](DISORDER:DIS008)) range from 2 to 3.9 per 1000 live births or children [1-8]. As [CP](DISORDER:DIS008) can cause disturbances in cognitive development and motor disorders, [dysarthria](DISORDER:DIS001) is one of the problems that [CP](DISORDER:DIS008) speakers frequently suffer from in communication [9-11]. Therefore, research pertaining to [CP](DISORDER:DIS008) speech has focused on a variety of linguistic aspects from a cross-linguistic perspective, including vowels, [speaking rates](SPEECH_FEATURE:SPF004), [prosody](SPEECH_FEATURE:SPF005), segmental error patterns, consonantal productions, literacy development, etc. (c.f., [10,12-18] among many others), with the aim of discovering the defining factors for those patients' [speech intelligibility](SPEECH_FEATURE:SPF001). Results from the endeavor revealed that, among other critical factors, the quality of consonantal productions plays a significant role in the [speech intelligibility](SPEECH_FEATURE:SPF001) of children and adults with [CP](DISORDER:DIS008) [18-26].

