### pubmed_36892950

Purpose: Early identification of speech motor involvement (SMI) in children with [cerebral palsy](DISORDER:DIS008) ([CP](DISORDER:DIS008)) is difficult because of overlapping features with many aspects of typical speech development. Quantitative measures of [speech intelligibility](SPEECH_FEATURE:SPF001) have the potential to differentiate between children with SMI and those with no SMI (NSMI). We examined thresholds for [speech intelligibility](SPEECH_FEATURE:SPF001) development in children with [CP](DISORDER:DIS008) relative to the low end of age-specific typical developmental expectations. We sought to determine whether there were [intelligibility](SPEECH_FEATURE:SPF001) differences between children with [CP](DISORDER:DIS008) and NSMI versus typically developing (TD) age-mates across the range of development and whether there were differences between children with [CP](DISORDER:DIS008) who have NSMI and those with [CP](DISORDER:DIS008) who have SMI across the range of development based on [speech intelligibility](SPEECH_FEATURE:SPF001).

Entities: cerebral palsy (DISORDER), CP (DISORDER), speech intelligibility (SPEECH_FEATURE), speech intelligibility (SPEECH_FEATURE), CP (DISORDER), intelligibility (SPEECH_FEATURE), CP (DISORDER), CP (DISORDER), CP (DISORDER), speech intelligibility (SPEECH_FEATURE)

